<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The material ladder (system: crafting.materialladder): the ores a settlement digs and the metals it smelts
    out of them, era by era. Its own file, per CLAUDE.md; BaseMineral is Items_Minerals.xml's parent, which
    resolves across files.

    THE RIMWORLD IDEA, AND WHY IT IS NOT PORTED 1:1
    RimWorld's metal economy is one step: mine compacted steel, get Steel (Buildings_Natural.xml, mineableYield
    40), and an electric smelter recovers Steel from slag (ExtractMetalFromSlag, 1 ChunkSlagSteel -> 15 Steel,
    workAmount 400; both read from Core's Defs). That fits a colony that lands with industrial technology. It is
    wrong for a settlement that has to reach steel through six eras: with steel lying in every hillside, the
    bronze age had no bronze and a neolithic smithy made steel knives. So the vein is ore and the metal is made,
    at a bench that belongs to its era:

      era         raw                 bench              made                 research
      sticks      flint (mined)       smithy (knapping)  flint knife          StoneTools
      bronze      copper ore          smelting furnace   copper               Copper
      bronze      copper + tin        smelting furnace   bronze               Bronze
      bronze      copper ore alone    smelting furnace   arsenical bronze     Bronze  (when there is no tin)
      classical   iron ore            bloomery           iron                 Iron
      medieval    iron                bloomery           steel (cementation)  Steel
      industrial  iron ore            electric smelter   steel                Electricity + BessemerProcess

    The recipes are RecipeDefs/Recipes_Smelting.xml, the benches ThingDefs_Buildings/Buildings_Smelting.xml.

    STUFF
    Copper (Items_Minerals.xml), bronze, iron and steel are all Metallic stuff, so each can be a wall, a door, a
    bench or a blade, exactly as RimWorld's Steel/Silver/Gold are. The numbers that rank them:
      SharpDamageMultiplier  RimWorld: steel 1 (the anchor), silver 0.85, gold 0.75. Copper 0.8, bronze 0.9 and
                             iron 0.95 are this port's, UNSOURCED, set in that family so the ladder climbs:
                             work-hardened bronze takes a better edge than copper, and bloomery iron only just
                             beats it — iron's advantage was that there was so much more of it.
      MaxHitPoints factor    RimWorld: steel 1, silver 0.7, gold 0.6. Copper 0.7, bronze 0.85, iron 0.9 are
                             this port's, UNSOURCED, same reasoning.
      commonality            RimWorld's meaning: how bulk a material is (steel 1, silver and plasteel 0.05).
                             Copper and bronze 0.3 — a bronze-age settlement casts blades from it, not walls;
                             iron 1, as steel. Crafting.MaterialLadder reads it to decide what a wall may be
                             built of.
      MarketValue            steel 1.9 is shipped content; the rest are UNSOURCED and only ordered
                             (ore < iron < copper < steel < bronze, bronze being the alloy of a scarce metal).
    The tests assert the ladder's order, never these literals.

    Tin stays a raw commodity (Items_Minerals.xml): cassiterite was co-smelted with copper, never worked alone.
  -->

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>CopperOre</defName>
    <label>copper ore</label>
    <description>Green-stained malachite and copper-bearing rock. Worthless until it has been through a furnace.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>0.5</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>IronOre</defName>
    <label>iron ore</label>
    <description>Heavy red-brown rock. There is iron in half the hills in the world; the trick was a fire hot enough to get it out.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>0.5</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
    </statBases>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Bronze</defName>
    <label>bronze</label>
    <description>Copper hardened with a tenth part of tin, or with the arsenic its own ore carried. Takes an edge copper never could, and casts.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>2.2</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      <SharpDamageMultiplier>0.9</SharpDamageMultiplier>
    </statBases>
    <stuffProps>
      <categories><li>Metallic</li></categories>
      <commonality>0.3</commonality>
      <statFactors>
        <MaxHitPoints>0.85</MaxHitPoints>
        <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      </statFactors>
    </stuffProps>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="BaseMineral">
    <defName>Iron</defName>
    <label>iron</label>
    <description>Wrought iron hammered out of a bloom. No harder than good bronze, but dug from any hillside rather than traded for across a sea.</description>
    <thingCategories><li>ResourcesRaw</li><li>Metals</li></thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>1.5</MarketValue>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      <SharpDamageMultiplier>0.95</SharpDamageMultiplier>
    </statBases>
    <stuffProps>
      <categories><li>Metallic</li></categories>
      <commonality>1</commonality>
      <statFactors>
        <MaxHitPoints>0.9</MaxHitPoints>
        <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      </statFactors>
    </stuffProps>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
